            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The east carries a dual valence in biblical interpretation: it is primarily the direction of God's presence, Eden, light, and Christ's return (advent/resurrection), but also secondarily represents exile, alienation from God, and destructive judgment (such as the east wind).\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The east symbolizes the 'front,' the spatial and temporal direction from which beginnings (Eden) and returns (God's glory, Christ's coming) originate.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Direction of God's presence, Eden, and Christ's return (positive); direction of exile and destructive judgment (negative).\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"The front; the source of beginnings and returns.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"BOOK_NARROWS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"BOOK_SUBSET\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book restricts the symbol to its positive, architectural, and temporal aspects (beginnings and returns), excluding the prominent negative biblical associations with exile, alienation, and judgment (e.g., the east wind).\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"None. The book's conceptual unification of the positive elements perfectly aligns with the consensus's primary strand.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book elegantly summarizes the positive consensus elements (Eden, the Temple's east gate, Christ's return) under the spatial-temporal concept of the 'front' (rooted in the Hebrew 'qedem'). However, by completely omitting the well-attested negative symbolism of eastward exile and judgment, it narrows the broader consensus extension.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"Ignoring the negative valence of the east leaves out a major biblical motif, such as humanity's eastward expulsion from Eden, the eastward migration to Babel, and the destructive nature of the east wind.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
